cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use PySyzygos, please cite it as below. Parts of the code and documentation were
  developed with the help of Claude (Anthropic), an AI model, using Claude Code; all results
  were reviewed and validated by the author.
title: "PySyzygos: predicting stellar occultations by asteroids, planets and moons for observer sites"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Zahl
    given-names: Percy
    alias: pyzahl
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23149371
version: "1.3.2"
repository-code: "https://github.com/pyzahl/PySyzygos"
abstract: >-
  Occultation predictor in Python (called PyOccult up to version 0.18; syzygos, Greek
  "yoked together": observer, asteroid and star in one line): finds asteroid occultations of Gaia DR3 stars for an observer site (JPL Horizons orbits, NAIF SPICE,
  a local magnitude-limited Gaia catalog), with an all-asteroid target pick, shadow paths
  with 1- and 3-sigma limits, event previews and a local web interface; also occultations
  by planets and their moons with disappearance and reappearance times. Validated against
  Occult Watcher Cloud and Occult as independent references.
keywords:
  - asteroid occultation
  - Gaia DR3
  - SPICE
  - IOTA
  - occultation prediction
  - JPL Horizons
  - Python
references:
  - type: data
    title: "Gaia DR3 star catalogs for PySyzygos: G <= 16 and G <= 18 subsets and a high-RUWE supplement (NumPy format)"
    authors:
      - family-names: Zahl
        given-names: Percy
    identifiers:
      - type: doi
        value: 10.5281/zenodo.23113337
